      "id": 1,
      "text": "Indian River was platted in 1879 where two rivers and lakes meet, marking the start of the community.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://michigan4you.com/history-of-indian-river-michigan-1900s-tourism/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In the 1880s the Grand Rapids & Indiana Railroad ran through Indian River, bringing lumbermen, settlers, and the first summer visitors.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6,
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://michigan4you.com/history-of-indian-river-michigan-1900s-tourism/",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Pinehurst Inn, erected in the early 1900s, remains a beloved landmark along the waterfront.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://michigan4you.com/history-of-indian-river-michigan-1900s-tourism/",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Indian River hosts the National Shrine of the Cross in the Woods, an open‑air sanctuary honoring Native American saint Kateri Tekakwitha.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Indian_River%2C_Michigan",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The Inland Waterway that links Burt Lake and Mullett Lake still attracts boaters, and a historical marker in Burt Lake State Park commemorates its legacy.",
